    "result_text": "The full suite passes: **277 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-city owner cap on `POST /api/owners`: creating an owner is rejected with **400** once the owner's city already contains 8 owners.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (mirroring the existing `RejectDailyOwnerLimit` pattern), I added:\n\n1. **`RejectCityOwnerLimit.java`** \u2014 the reject step. Counts existing owners whose city matches (case-insensitively), and throws `CityOwnerLimitExceededException` when the count is already \u2265 8 (`MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY`). Null-city safe.\n\n2. **`CityOwnerLimitExceededException.java`** \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause.\n\n3. **`CityOwnerLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 global escalation handler responding 400 Bad Request with a `ProblemDetail`.\n\n4. **`...CityOwnerLimitExceededException.yml`** \u2014 wires the exception to its handler under `officefloor/escalation/`.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `rejectCityLimit` step right after `normalizeCity` (so the city is in canonical spelling before counting) and before `rejectDuplicate`, all under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\nThe pre-existing `Cp14Tests.coreRejectsOverCityCap` acceptance test (8 owners in a fresh city, 9th expects 400) now passes, along with the entire test suite.",
